WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.080
Trust boundaries are points where data, identity, permissions, or assumptions change.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:20.180
AI systems have several important boundaries: user input, external documents, web pages, emails, tickets, retrieved resources, tool responses, model responses, third-party integrations, and downstream systems.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.800
A safe architecture distinguishes trusted configuration from untrusted content.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:31.260
System instructions and application policy should not be treated the same way as text retrieved from a document or entered by a user.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.200
Threat modeling should separate the instruction path, the data path, and the action path.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:44.500
The instruction path includes system prompts, user prompts, tool descriptions, and external content that may influence model behavior.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.400
The data path includes files, retrieved documents, memory, logs, and tool responses.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:01:00.380
The action path includes tool calls, workflow triggers, database changes, messages, tickets, cloud actions, and other effects outside the model.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.960
Prompt injection and indirect prompt injection are useful high-level concepts for threat modeling.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:12.340
The stable lesson is that untrusted content may try to influence model behavior when the AI system reads or retrieves it.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.520
This course does not need payloads to teach the principle.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.100
Retrieved content should be treated as data, not authority.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:25.400
It should not override policy, grant permissions, disable controls, or decide what tools the system may use.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:33.620
AI threat modeling should ask direct questions: what can the model read, what can it remember, what can it decide, and what can it do?

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:42.880
It should also consider data leakage, excessive agency, tool misuse, model error, hallucination, third-party dependency risk, and unclear ownership.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:50.700
The outcome should be concrete controls such as access filtering, validation, approval gates, monitoring, and response procedures.
